---
// "Full-Bleed, Screen-Graded" hero — Riff 14 of the dev-services pivot
// exploration (see docs/HeroDesigns). Same full-bleed photo, broadcast
// grade (scanlines, screen vignette, warmth) and real animated haze as the
// prior bottom-left version, but the content block now sits right-of-
// center, left-aligned, slightly overlapping Doug's body, carrying the
// pivoted copy plus a services list and two CTAs instead of just a name.
// The photo's own baked-in haze (upper-right of hero.webp) drifts with a
// stronger, faster amplitude than a purely decorative hero needs, since
// it's now sharing the frame with the content block instead of being the
// only thing happening in it. Four "now playing" chips drift up out of
// the bell on curved paths, each naming a real track from Listen.astro. A
// mobile-first pass tried folding the chips and the terminal pill into
// .hero__content's flow instead of hiding them outright on phones - real
// content, not filler - but on an actual phone it was simply too much
// competing for the same short viewport (confirmed via a real screenshot,
// not assumed: the headline, eyebrow, chips, and terminal all stacked up
// and crowded/overlapped). Reverted to display:none on phones (see the
// max-width: 640px query) for the chips, terminal, AND the eyebrow, which
// wasn't part of the earlier reactive-hide list but is cut for the same
// reason - the phone hero keeps the headline, services, and two CTAs only.
const tracks = [
  { href: 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00G9Y8TPE', label: 'Better Than TV' },
  { href: 'https://www.amazon.com/Goran-Ivanovic-Group/dp/B000HWY64C', label: 'Goran Ivanovic Group' },
  { href: 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B006HTEKHM', label: 'Underwater' },
  { href: 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00169L616', label: 'Folk Tales' },
];
---

<header id="hero" class="hero">
  <img class="hero__photo" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}images/hero.webp`} alt="Doug Rosenberg holding a saxophone" />
  <div class="hero__haze" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="hero__scan" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="hero__screen-vignette" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {tracks.map((track, i) => (
    <a
      class={`hero__chip hero__chip--nc${i + 1}${i % 2 === 1 ? ' hero__chip--mirror' : ''}`}
      href={track.href}
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    ><span>♪</span> {track.label}</a>
  ))}

  <div class="hero__content wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow hero__eyebrow">Formerly on stage. Now behind the screen.</p>
    <h1 class="hero__title">I make websites<br />that <span class="accent">sound like you.</span></h1>
    <div class="hero__services">
      <div class="hero__service"><span class="hero__service-bar"></span>Web Design</div>
      <div class="hero__service"><span class="hero__service-bar"></span>Development</div>
      <div class="hero__service"><span class="hero__service-bar"></span>Branding</div>
      <div class="hero__service"><span class="hero__service-bar"></span>Ongoing Support</div>
    </div>

    <div class="hero__ctas">
      <a href="https://dougrosenbergdev.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="cta cta-primary" data-cta="hero-primary">Like This Site? Let's Build Yours →</a>
      <a href="https://dougrosenbergdev.com/webdesign" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="cta cta-secondary" data-cta="hero-secondary">See My Work</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Sits in the empty lower-left of the frame. Used to rotate through
       four destinations (listen/lessons/private events/portfolio) as a
       typewriter cycle; simplified to a static line once the new #dev
       section gave "portfolio" a real home with its own CTAs - the other
       two rotating items were already hard to discover (a visitor had to
       catch the right moment in a 10s cycle), so dropping the rotation
       loses little and removes a third competing visual idiom (terminal/
       typewriter) from a hero that already carries broadcast and musical-
       notation motifs. Full rotating version archived at
       ui-lab/artifacts/dougrosenbergmusic/hero-terminal-widget.html. -->
  <a class="hero__terminal" href="#listen">
    <span class="hero__terminal-prompt" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span>
    <span class="hero__terminal-text">listen now  →</span><span class="hero__terminal-cursor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </a>
</header>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .hero__photo {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 14%;
    /* At this crop's aspect ratio the box is wider than the photo, so
       object-fit: cover already stretches it to full width with zero
       horizontal slack left to pan with object-position - only the
       vertical axis has room. A small scale-up manufactures that slack so
       the subsequent translateX can actually move him left, away from
       the content block; object-position's Y value was pulled back from
       20% to 14% to give the scale's symmetric crop room to grow without
       clipping the hat brim. */
    transform: scale(1.08) translateX(-5%);
    filter: sepia(0.18) saturate(1.08) brightness(1.22) contrast(1.06);
  }

  /* the studio haze already baked into hero.webp (right side of the frame)
     is static pixels - this drifts two soft blurred glows over that same
     region so it reads as living smoke instead of a still photo. Tuned
     stronger/faster than a purely decorative hero needs, since this haze
     now shares the frame with the content block instead of being the only
     thing happening in it. */
  .hero__haze {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .hero__haze::before,
  .hero__haze::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(40px);
  }

  .hero__haze::before {
    width: 42vw;
    height: 42vw;
    top: 8%;
    right: 6%;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(224, 121, 79, 0.28), transparent 70%);
    animation: haze-drift-a 13s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  .hero__haze::after {
    width: 38vw;
    height: 38vw;
    top: 28%;
    right: 14%;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(82, 209, 166, 0.22), transparent 70%);
    animation: haze-drift-b 15s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes haze-drift-a {
    0%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0) scale(1); }
    50% { transform: translate(-7%, 8%) scale(1.28); }
  }

  @keyframes haze-drift-b {
    0%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0) scale(1); }
    50% { transform: translate(8%, -6%) scale(0.82); }
  }

  /* the broadcast grade — scanlines and a soft screen-shaped vignette,
     laid over the full photo instead of shrinking it into a frame */
  .hero__scan {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05) 0 1px, transparent 1px 3px
    );
    mix-blend-mode: overlay;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .hero__screen-vignette {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 18vw 4vw rgba(6, 9, 15, 0.55);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .hero__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.1) 0%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.25) 55%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.85) 100%),
      radial-gradient(ellipse 62% 58% at 68% 52%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.6), transparent 72%);
  }

  /* content block sits right-of-center, left-aligned, slightly
     overlapping Doug's body rather than hugging the frame's edge */
  .hero__content {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    left: 50%;
    right: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    max-width: none;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .hero__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
  }

  .hero__title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 3.6vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    margin: 0;
    /* smoke-tinted headline, shimmering within the site's own glow
       family rather than a second competing hue */
    background: linear-gradient(100deg, var(--text) 0%, var(--glow) 32%, var(--text) 58%, #9fe8cd 80%, var(--text) 100%);
    background-size: 300% 100%;
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    animation: fade-up 0.7s ease-out 0.15s both, smoke-text 16s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  .hero__title .accent {
    background: none;
    -webkit-background-clip: initial;
    background-clip: initial;
    color: var(--glow);
    -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--glow);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  @keyframes fade-up {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(14px); }
    to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }

  @keyframes smoke-text {
    0%, 100% { background-position: 0% 50%; }
    50% { background-position: 100% 50%; }
  }

  .hero__services {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    margin: 1.6rem 0;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .hero__service {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    /* Bespoke, not --text-muted (0.62) - this sits over a hazy photo
       rather than a flat --bg, where that opacity reads as low-contrast
       and hard to read. Bumped locally rather than raising the shared
       token, which other --text-muted usages elsewhere rely on staying
       quiet. */
    color: rgba(233, 237, 245, 0.85);
  }

  .hero__service-bar {
    width: 26px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--glow);
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  .hero__ctas {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  /* skewed "command line" — static now (see the markup comment above for
     why the rotating version was simplified out). Sized to its content,
     not a fixed box, so it still reads as a pill rather than a full-width
     bar. */
  .hero__terminal {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    left: 5%;
    bottom: 10%;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.8rem 1.4rem;
    background: rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.72);
    border: 1px solid rgba(233, 237, 245, 0.18);
    border-radius: 14px;
    backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
    transform: rotate(-6deg);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--text);
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
    transition: border-color 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;
  }

  .hero__terminal:hover,
  .hero__terminal:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--glow);
    transform: rotate(-6deg) scale(1.03);
  }

  .hero__terminal:focus-visible {
    outline: none;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
  }

  .hero__terminal-prompt {
    color: var(--glow);
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .hero__terminal-cursor {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 0.5em;
    height: 1em;
    background: var(--glow);
    flex-shrink: 0;
    animation: terminal-blink 1s step-end infinite;
  }

  @keyframes terminal-blink {
    0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
    50% { opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* "now playing" chips — real track titles (see Listen.astro), drifting
     up from the bell one at a time on a gentle curved path rather than a
     straight vertical line; alternating chips curve opposite ways so they
     don't all bow the same direction. Each links out to its album, so
     (unlike a purely decorative chip) it needs to be reachable and
     visible on keyboard focus even mid-animation, and needs to actually
     render for reduced-motion users instead of freezing at the
     animation's invisible starting frame. */
  .hero__chip {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.7rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: rgba(19, 26, 38, 0.75);
    border: 1px solid var(--glow-soft);
    backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    color: var(--text);
    text-decoration: none;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(14px) scale(0.9);
    animation: chip-float 9s ease-in-out infinite;
    white-space: nowrap;
    transition: border-color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .hero__chip:hover,
  .hero__chip:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--brass);
  }

  .hero__chip:focus-visible {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    animation-play-state: paused;
    outline: none;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
  }

  .hero__chip span {
    color: var(--glow);
  }

  .hero__chip--nc1 { left: 27%; top: 34%; animation-delay: 0s; }
  .hero__chip--nc2 { left: 14%; top: 21%; animation-delay: 4.5s; }
  .hero__chip--nc3 { left: 22%; top: 45%; animation-delay: 2.25s; }
  .hero__chip--nc4 { left: 10%; top: 30%; animation-delay: 6.75s; }

  @keyframes chip-float {
    0%, 100% { opacity: 0; transform: translate(0, 14px) scale(0.9); }
    8% { opacity: 1; transform: translate(3px, 2px) scale(1); }
    22% { opacity: 1; transform: translate(11px, -12px) scale(1); }
    38% { opacity: 1; transform: translate(5px, -28px) scale(1); }
    48% { opacity: 0; transform: translate(-6px, -44px) scale(0.95); }
  }

  @keyframes chip-float-mirror {
    0%, 100% { opacity: 0; transform: translate(0, 14px) scale(0.9); }
    8% { opacity: 1; transform: translate(-3px, 2px) scale(1); }
    22% { opacity: 1; transform: translate(-11px, -12px) scale(1); }
    38% { opacity: 1; transform: translate(-5px, -28px) scale(1); }
    48% { opacity: 0; transform: translate(6px, -44px) scale(0.95); }
  }

  .hero__chip--mirror {
    animation-name: chip-float-mirror;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hero__chip,
    .hero__title,
    .hero__eyebrow,
    .hero__terminal-cursor,
    .hero__haze::before,
    .hero__haze::after {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  /* tablet: drop the overlap, pull the block into a plain right-hand
     column instead of jumping straight to a full mobile stack. The "now
     playing" chips are hidden from here down too - their left/top percentages
     are tuned for the desktop absolute-overlay composition and land
     straight on top of the content block's text once .hero__content drops
     into normal flow.

     A fixed max-width: 460px looked fine at 768px (where it was originally
     checked) but was never re-checked across the rest of this range - at
     the narrow end (641px) that same 460px box is nearly the full viewport
     width, and left-aligned text inside it starts right where Doug is
     (confirmed via a real 641px screenshot, not assumed). min(460px, 58vw)
     keeps the original 460px wherever there's room for it (~793px+, where
     the headline still wants that width to hold "that sound like you." on
     one line - dropping the cap to 400px fixed 641px but broke this,
     orphaning "you." onto its own third line at 1024px) and only shrinks
     below 460px on the narrower widths where 58vw is the smaller number.

     text-align: right alone (the fix already proven at the phone
     breakpoint below) turned out not to be enough here on its own: the
     eyebrow sentence is naturally almost exactly as wide as the box, so a
     right-aligned single line of it still starts at the box's own left
     edge either way (confirmed via a second real screenshot after the
     first fix attempt still showed it landing on him) - same root issue as
     the phone breakpoint's eyebrow overlap, same fix: cap the eyebrow's
     own width so it wraps to two shorter lines that actually pull clear,
     instead of relying on the container alone. */
  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    .hero__chip {
      display: none;
    }
    /* Lighthouse mobile audit: 50/100, traced to ~5.9s of paint/composite
       work under throttled CPU (desktop: 95/100, no such cost - so this is
       purely a lower-end-hardware concern, not a real bug). The chips above
       and the staff-nav's breathing dots are already display:none at this
       width, so they're already free; these three are what's actually left
       running - the haze blobs' drift, the title's shimmer, and the
       terminal's cursor blink. Freezing them to their resting frame (not
       hiding the elements - the haze glow, the gradient title, and the
       cursor block all still render, just without the continuous
       repaint/recomposite) keeps the atmosphere while cutting the
       measured cost. Desktop keeps the full animated version untouched. */
    .hero__haze::before,
    .hero__haze::after {
      animation: none;
    }
    .hero__title {
      animation: fade-up 0.7s ease-out 0.15s both;
    }
    .hero__terminal-cursor {
      animation: none;
    }
    .hero__content {
      position: relative;
      left: auto;
      right: auto;
      top: auto;
      transform: none;
      /* .hero__content also carries the global .wrap class (max-width:
         1180px; margin: 0 auto - a page-content gutter meant for normal
         in-flow sections). Inside .hero's flex row, that auto margin wins
         over .hero's own justify-content: flex-end (flexbox gives auto
         margins first claim on free space), so the block was actually
         being centered - equal gaps on both sides - not hugging the right
         edge the way every other breakpoint does. Confirmed via computed
         styles at 665x674 (Doug's "weird size" report): a 132px margin on
         each side, not the flush-right position the max-width/text-align
         tuning above assumed it already had. Zeroing it here restores the
         actual right-hug and moves everything further right at once,
         which is what "slide the text right" needed - not another narrower
         max-width. */
      margin: 0;
      max-width: min(460px, 58vw);
      padding-bottom: 0;
      text-align: right;
    }
    .hero__eyebrow {
      max-width: 13rem;
      margin-left: auto;
    }
    .hero__services {
      align-items: flex-end;
    }
    .hero__ctas {
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }
    .hero__scrim {
      background:
        linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.1) 0%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.2) 60%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.85) 100%),
        linear-gradient(to left, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.82) 0%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.5) 32%, transparent 62%);
    }
  }

  /* Phone: the content block was vertically centered over the full-bleed
     photo like every wider breakpoint, which on a narrow/short viewport
     put the title and services list directly over Doug's face instead of
     beside it - unreadable and cluttered (confirmed via screenshot, not
     assumed). Anchoring it to the bottom instead reuses the scrim's
     already-strong bottom darkening, keeps his face fully visible above
     it, and leaves it a plain dark band to sit on regardless of how many
     lines the title wraps to at any given width.

     That fixed the vertical crowding but not the horizontal - .hero's own
     `justify-content: flex-end` is what keeps the content block right-of-
     center (clear of Doug, who sits left-of-center in the photo) at every
     other breakpoint, but `max-width: 100%` here made the block fill the
     entire row, leaving justify-content nothing to push against. Left-
     aligned text in a full-width block then started flush at the hero's
     left edge - directly over his face again (confirmed via a real
     448x487 screenshot, not assumed). Right-aligning the text itself,
     rather than fighting for width, is the fix that survives regardless
     of exact viewport width or how many lines anything wraps to. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero__terminal {
      display: none;
    }
    .hero__content {
      max-width: 100%;
      align-self: flex-end;
      padding-bottom: 2.75rem;
      text-align: right;
    }
    /* Cut outright rather than shrunk to fit - a phone-width hero has room
       for the headline, services, and the two CTAs; the eyebrow sentence
       was one more line competing with the headline for the same tight
       vertical space directly above it (confirmed via a real screenshot:
       folding it plus the chips/terminal into flow read as crowded, not
       just tight). */
    .hero__eyebrow {
      display: none;
    }
    .hero__services {
      align-items: flex-end;
    }
    .hero__ctas {
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }
    .hero__scrim {
      background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.05) 0%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.4) 42%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.88) 62%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.95) 100%);
    }
  }
</style>
